Q: How to get all data from array? A: <p>In CodeIgniter, it is recommended to use the framework's dedicated <code>$this-&gt;input-&gt;post()</code> method to access POST request data.</p>
<p>Every implementation of a &quot;common model&quot; in a CodeIgniter application has appeared to me as a symptom of bad MVC design. The model becomes a dumping grown for completely unrelated table interactions. A model should be split into its logical concerns so that interactions can be simply managed without getting tangled.</p>
<p>Generally, a controller method should not be printing data, that is responsibility of the view layer.</p>
<p>Your coding attempt seems to guarantee that the two expected fields will be populated with the same number of elements. This means that transposing the data into pairs can be elegantly done with <code>array_map()</code>.</p>
<pre class="lang-php prettyprint-override"><code>class CommonController extends CI_Controller
{
    public function __construct()
    {
        parent::__construct();
        $this-&gt;load-&gt;model('common_model'); //load your model my model is &quot;common model&quot;
    }

    public function add_work()
    {
        $pairs = array_map(
            function ($name, $work) {
                $pair = compact('name', 'work');
                $this-&gt;common_model-&gt;insert($pair);
                return $pair;
            }
            $this-&gt;input-&gt;post('name'),
            $this-&gt;input-&gt;post('work')
        );
        $this-&gt;load-&gt;view('work.php', ['pairs' =&gt; $pairs]);
    }
}
</code></pre>
<p>View:</p>
<pre class="lang-php prettyprint-override"><code>foreach ($pairs as $pair) {
    echo &quot;&lt;p&gt;Name: {$pair['name']}, Work: {$pair['work']}&lt;/p&gt;&quot;;
}
</code></pre>
<p>If your common_model's <code>insert()</code> query returns the autogenerated id from the database (hint: it should), then you could append that values as the third element of the <code>$pairs</code> array and show that in your view too.</p>
